# anon-terms — the denylist anon-guard scans outgoing contributions for.
#
# !!! GRIND ANON TRAP !!! — COPY THIS, NEVER FILL IT IN HERE.
# The filled-in list IS the confidential part (it enumerates your clients). It
# is machine-local and must never be committed:
#
#     cp scripts/grind/anon-terms.example ~/.claude/grindhouse.anon-terms
#     # then add your real terms to the COPY
#
# One extended regex per line, matched case-insensitively against ADDED lines
# only. `#` starts a comment. Keep entries tight enough not to false-positive on
# ordinary English (a client called "Core" would flag every third line).
#
# What belongs here:
#   - client and product names (incl. common misspellings / internal codenames)
#   - your employer + its domains
#   - private repo names, internal org names, ticket-tracker project prefixes
#   - teammates' names and handles
#
# Built-in patterns are ALWAYS on and need no entry here: e-mail addresses,
# absolute user paths, credentials/tokens, private IP ranges, and the usual
# internal-only hostname suffixes. See the pattern list in anon-guard itself.

# --- examples (delete these; they match nothing real) ---
# acme-?corp
# \bWidgetCo\b
# projectbluebird
# \bPROJ-[0-9]+\b
# yourcompany\.(com|io)
# github\.com/your-org/
